## 3. Honesty discipline

These ship seeded because each is a measured failure class rather than a taste.
They are all one idea in different costumes: **a surface must not be able to
report something it did not establish.**

**3.1 A status or diagnostic never returns green on an empty, failed or
malformed read.** Three states, never two: **ok**, **problem**, **unknown**. A
read that throws, times out, returns nothing, or returns something unparseable
yields `unknown` and renders distinctly. Wrapping a read in a broad handler that
returns an empty collection converts "could not look" into "looked, found
nothing wrong" - and the failure becomes invisible exactly when it matters.
*Check:* make the source unreadable in a test and assert the surface does not
read green.

**3.2 Honest absence, measured zero and failure are three different things.**
"No records exist", "the measurement ran and the answer is 0", and "the
measurement did not run" must be distinguishable in the data, not only in a log
line. A renderer that maps all three to `0` is lying in two of the three cases.
Carry the distinction in the type - an optional value, a status field, a
sentinel that is not a number - and never let a default fill it in silently.

**3.3 A fallback whose output can equal its input is not a fallback, for
anything that produces variety.** If a generator of distinct values - names,
keys, ids, sampled variants, colours, orderings - can fall back to a constant,
the feature has two indistinguishable states: working, and not running at all.
Either raise, or return something **visibly marked** as a fallback and record it
as such. *Check:* empty the generator's source and assert it refuses or marks;
then assert two successive calls against a populated source differ.

**3.4 Two verbs that reach the same end state share the same preconditions.**
If `archive()` and `close()` both end with the record closed, they validate the
same things, emit the same records, and refuse in the same cases. A second path
to a state is where the guard gets forgotten - one route checks permission and
the other does not, and the difference is invisible until somebody takes the
other route. *Check:* when you add a path to an existing state, list every
existing path to it and diff their preconditions line by line.

**3.5 Platform-varying spellings are derived in tests, never hardcoded.**
Exception class names, path separators, line endings, locale-formatted numbers,
temp directory layouts and error message text vary by platform and by version. A
test asserting a literal spelling passes where it was written and fails
somewhere else, which reads as a real defect and costs somebody an afternoon.
Derive the expected value from the same source the code uses - catch the
exception type rather than matching its name, join paths rather than writing a
separator, compare parsed values rather than formatted strings.

**3.6 Persisted positions use durable keys, never bare ordinals.** State that
points into an ordered collection stores the identity of the thing - a name, a
slug, a stable id - not index 7. An ordinal is meaningful only relative to a
list that will be reordered, and when it is, nothing errors: the system simply
resumes somewhere else. Where an ordinal must be kept for compatibility, version
the state format and resolve key-first with the ordinal as a clamped fallback.
*Check:* reorder the collection in a test and assert restored state still points
at the same item.

**3.7 <PROJECT_SPECIFIC_HONESTY_RULE>.** <Add yours here as they are learned.
Every entry names the check that catches the next one.>

